<!-- GridWindow: the merged "Continue" screen (lib/ui/gridwindow.py).

     ONE ROW PER BAND, ALL ON ONE SCREEN. Each band (resume / next up /
     watched / library) has its own labelled header and its own
     horizontally-scrolling poster row, and the rows are stacked in a
     vertical `grouplist` (30001). This is Kodi's own idiom for sectioned
     content: skin.estuary's Home builds its widget rows exactly this way
     (`WidgetListPoster` inside `WidgetGroupListCommon`, each preceded by
     a `CategoryLabel`).

     Two earlier attempts are recorded so they are not retried:
       - Header rows interleaved into ONE wrapping `panel`. A panel gives
         every row the item layout's full height, so each header cost a
         whole poster row of screen.
       - One band at a time behind a tab strip. It hid three quarters of
         the screen to label four groups.

     LAYOUT, after Netflix/Apple TV. A 256x384 poster (a true 2:3, in the
     ~250 range those apps use at 1080p) in a 280x470 cell, six of them
     across from the standard 96px left margin. The row is deliberately
     wider than the frame so the sixth poster bleeds off the right edge:
     a row that stops short of the edge looks finished, one that runs off
     it reads as "there is more this way", which is exactly the Netflix
     cue. Vertically the first row is whole, the second nearly so, and the
     next is cut by the frame - the same "more below" cue, and the reason
     the stack is not squeezed to fit every band at once.

     Focus is a 6% zoom plus an accent underline rather than a border box
     around the artwork, again following those apps: the tile lifts toward
     the viewer instead of being caged, and nothing overlaps the poster.

     `usecontrolcoords` is what makes the stack work: each control is laid
     out under the previous one, so a row whose list is empty collapses
     entirely - both its header and its panel are `visible` on that list's
     own NumItems. A viewer with nothing finished sees no "Recently
     watched" heading at all, rather than an empty band.

     THE CELL LAYOUTS ARE GENERATED: all four rows carry a byte-identical
     copy, because this skin has no Includes.xml to share one through.
     Keep them in sync - tests/test_gridwindow.py asserts they are.

     The poster box is a true 2:3, so `aspectratio=keep` draws a standard
     poster edge to edge inside it. That is what lets the 256px progress
     track and the labels line up with the ARTWORK rather than with a
     wider box - at an earlier 240 box the rendered poster was only 216
     wide and the bar overhung it by 12px a side.
     `gridwindow.PROGRESS_TRACK_WIDTH` must equal that 256.

     Band identity is carried by colour as well as by the headers - the
     badge is tinted per band (gridwindow._BAND_COLOURS), the header takes
     the same tint, and finished titles are drawn dimmed - so a card still
     says which band it belongs to once its header has scrolled off. -->
